WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.551
Secure randomness and repeatable tests pull in different directions.

2
00:00:03.551 --> 00:00:07.497
Tests often need deterministic behavior so failures can be reproduced.

3
00:00:07.497 --> 00:00:09.470
Production security values need unpredictability.

4
00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:15.389
A good design separates the random source interface from the code that consumes random values.

5
00:00:15.389 --> 00:00:24.465
The consuming code can be tested with a deterministic source in a controlled test context, while production wiring uses the approved CSPRNG source

6
00:00:24.465 --> 00:00:26.043
and rejects weak modes.

7
00:00:26.043 --> 00:00:28.016
This separation should be intentional.

8
00:00:28.016 --> 00:00:34.330
Test hooks, fixed seeds, debug generators, and deterministic modes must not be reachable accidentally in production.

9
00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:40.249
Build configuration, dependency injection, feature flags, and environment-specific wiring should make the difference clear.

10
00:00:40.249 --> 00:00:48.930
If a test source is available, name it as test-only and restrict it to test binaries or explicitly controlled test configuration.

11
00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:54.454
Avoid designs where a missing production dependency silently causes a fallback to deterministic behavior.

12
00:00:54.454 --> 00:00:58.795
Tests should verify both the consumer logic and the production boundary.

13
00:00:58.795 --> 00:01:06.292
Consumer tests can check formatting, length, rejection of invalid ranges, collision handling paths, and error propagation using deterministic inputs.

14
00:01:06.292 --> 00:01:15.368
Integration or configuration tests can verify that production code uses the approved source and that weak sources are not enabled in release settings.

15
00:01:15.368 --> 00:01:18.919
This gives the team reproducibility without weakening operational security.

16
00:01:18.919 --> 00:01:27.600
The design goal is not random behavior in every test; it is clear separation between deterministic test evidence and unpredictable production values.
